at when put together by way of their  complex interactions  exhibit a new behavior that is called  that phenomenon is called emergence and  it's something that is unique to complex  systems  and something that that i think is quite  fascinating because  if we try to avoid complexity where we  can what we essentially end up doing is  we avoid  emergence we avoid the phenomena of  emergence emergence that also happens in  social networks if you look at  the arak spring for example you know  there there's some indication that that  twitter  played a role in that which is a role  that emerged  on top of that communication platform  that they built probably not foreseen  that it could ever have that impact  biological systems  are no different in that if you look at  you know the human body most of the  diseases that we are faced with today  can no longer be easily solved by  developing a drug that just  targets one particular spot in your body  if you look for instance that cancer  if you look at hiv those are highly  highly diverse diseases  that require a holistic system analysis  in order to understand  how to tackle them and that in turn  requires us to analyze and understand  complex systems  and lastly the financial system i think  we have we've had a very  uh vivid demonstration of what happens  when you don't understand complex  systems  you know that happened in in 2009 when  the financial system was on the risk of  collapse  and nobody really had a clue what the  hell was going on the reason that was  the case is because we did not and at  that time  didn't try to understand the  complexities of these different  financial dependencies between the  institutions  and therefore had no idea what was going  to happen when one of those  collapsed again pointing to the fact  that complex systems  are extremely important for us to  understand and analyze  and while we try to get away from  complexity where we can  i think a much more interesting approach  is now to ac