nd trying to figure  out if graphs could become  part of your day-to-day toolbox i think  if we want to solve these problems  graphs need to become  as natural to us are as as hash maps we  use hash maps on a daily basis we don't  really think much about them we know  when to use them and what they're good  for  for graphs that is not so easy maybe  just because we're not familiar with  them but hopefully we'll get to that  point  and you will be able to use them just  like you currently use a hash map  now one particular implementation of  this hash map or particular information  graph is titanium there are many others  and i encourage you to check them out  but i'm going to talk  a little more about titan and the  distributed graph database  titan itself was is inspired a great  deal  by the most amazing graph computer  that i know of and that that is the  human brain right it's the only graph  computer that we know if that  actually natively runs on hardware that  is amenable to graph computing  so we're kind of at a disadvantage as  computer science is trying to build  a graph based system because our  hardware is essentially sequential right  we're still  very close to the original turing  machine is that we have this one big  long tape  now the tape is you know partly in the  level of caches partly in ram and partly  on this  but it's still pretty much sequential  and doesn't have that connectedness  property that our brain has  but still i think we can make a good  good attempt at that and with with titan  we're trying to do that  titan partly tries to get around that  that problem of of  sequentiality that our phenomena  architecture imposes on us but  representing grass in an adjacency list  format  but there are three things that really  differentiate titan  from other systems that i know of  that are inspired by how the brain works  i want to talk about these three the  first is our brain  is a highly concurrent distributed  system  it's quite amazing how that works we're  not consciously aware of that  which is even more amazing but at any  point in time you have many many  processes that concurrently  happen in your brain and while most of  your brain is effectively  at sleep meaning not literally sleeping  but not  active right and so in building  a graph database we felt like one of the  things that we needed to build in from  the get-go  was that ability to be highly concurrent  and highly distributed  now we benefited greatly here from the  event of of  no sql which was you know really  concerned with building distributed  architectures that were fundamentally  scalable so we just  write piggyback on top of that using  cassandra apache cassandra and apache  hbase to build titan on top of  but there are some unique challenges to  building graph database and one is that  it's not quite so trivial how you want  to partition your data  how would you assign a graph to the  individual machines in a cluster  right how do you coerce a graph into  rows and columns which is ultimately the  bigtable  data model that you have now we said  we're going to use a  adjacency list representation that's one  thing but it still doesn't answer the  question for how do you assign the  individual vertices to individual  machines  that is a problem that is called graph  partitioning  and it's quite amazing how our brain  does that  it was long thought that the the  different parts of our brain was was  sort of had fixed assignment that there  was this motor brain and that there was  the visual cortex  and that basically that's how it went  right that's how the brain  by your genetic code would ever  represent you know it's it's  ever allocated computing resources now  it turned out though that when people  looked at blind people  that it's it's not true that their  visual cortex was just  sitting there not doing anything because  obviously you know since they were blind  it didn't have any input  it actually turned out that the brain  reallocated those computing resources  to other sensory systems and therefore  blind people have much  better sense much better senses when it  comes for instance to touch or smell  now that is quite fascinating how does  the brain do that it seems to indicate  that we don't have in it genetically  encoded the way that we that the brain  partitions its resources  but that there's some way of figuring  out and in other words that's something  that we needed to do  with titan as well is some way of  figuring out how to allocate  the particular machine resources to  particular types of parts of the graph  such that we still balance out overall  the computational resources  so that we don't have hot spots in our  system now it's a problem that  by no means i'm claiming to have solved  because it's quite challenging to do  when you have a real-time system that  receives  updates all the time right you don't you  never have a static view of the graph  that you can then go to town on and say  i'm going to cut this up in this way and  then store it on these machines so  approach that we take  is is a mixture of existing partitioning  algorithms and we  have used label propagation most  effectively to solve that problem  but combine it with an innate  understanding of how the graph works at  the data model and  allow titan to give it hints so that it  knows how to chunk up its data  and then use automatic methods like  label propagation to fine tune the  partitioning  and we found that to be a very effective  solution to solving this problem  and just hope to do much more research  along those lines to come up  with brain inspired petitioning  algorithms  another thing that our brain does quite  amazingly well  is figuring out what is relevant or not  i mean at any given second you're  bombarded with tons of information  and our brain seems to filter them out  pretty well so that you focus on the  most relevant ones  more amazingly even though you might  temporarily consume a lot of information  over time it seems that only the most  relevant stuff  survives if you look at neuroscience  people say well you know the things that  fire together wire together so the  things that you frequently use get  connected  and the connection gets stronger over  time as you use them and that's i think  a very important  principle but there's also this  principle of weighing or relevance  or as jeff hawkins wrote in his book the  brain has a strong sense of  sequence of time sequence time is a very  important dimension for us humans we  think of things as they occur  sequentially over time  but we tend to order a lot of other  things as well so that notion of  ordering in a graph is important and  one way that titan incorporates that is  by the by the notion of a vertex centric  index now what does that mean when you  look at a vertex in the graph you have  all these incoming and outgoing edges  that connectors are shown in this  picture here  now typically if you know a lot of  typically the way it would represent  them is you  look at all edges and create them equal  right and so then when you retrieve it  you have to go over all these edges  they're all equal so you have to iterate  over them and  then figure out which of these edges are  actually meaningful to you  what titan allows you to do it allows  you to specify  certain relevance criterion by which you  want to order those edges  and then index them accordingly so that  you can  say things like give me the edges that  have  the 10 edges that have the highest  timestamp time  again being a very important dimension  here by which you would order those  edges  right and titan will then lay out the  data in the underlying storage backend  so that it can efficiently retrieve only  those edges that you're actually asking  for  all right another way in which we enable  or in  which we build titan that is inspired by  the brain and turns out that that is  fundamentally important when you look at  scalable systems because as the amount  of data increases  each vertex the degree of each vertex  means the number of edges also increases  and so you quickly run out of space and  out of computational resources and  things just slow down a lot if you don't  have these index structures  and finally one of the things that our  brain does amazingly well as well  is that subconscious thinking  now you think when you sleep your brain  is asleep that turns out to be false in  fact our brain is anything but asleep  when we sleep it does a very important  job and that is organizing our brain  you receive a lot of information during  the day and at night your brain goes to  town on that information  trying to sort it into what you already  know in order to make sense of your  world  what i'm trying to do today really is to  implant in you that idea that grafts are  important so that when you go to sleep  tonight  it's going to subconsciously eat you up  from the inside and then you wake up to  morning like man i should learn more  about grass that's really what this talk  is all about  but that notion of real time processing  which we all are aware of that's our  day-to-day thinking that's how we go  about the world but also the  subconscious the lingering thought  i think is a very important analogy for  computing as well  we have titan which is a real-time  system that answers real-time  queries that you have you know for your  day-to-day applications or operations  but then we also built faunas which is  in a way titan's brother  and responsible for analytic type  processing the type of lingering thought  when you try to  analyze or rearrange your data it  operates directly on titan  and in fact uses the same query language  gremlin that i showed you so the way  that you think about the data is the  same  but the process behind it is different  one is real time the other is batch  one is oltp the other is olap but both  of them are married together  in an approach that is tightly  integrated you don't see any etl  pipeline going on here  right so so the the important aspect  here being putting those two together  so you can build applications to take  advantage of both your real-time  thinking  but also that lingering thought the  analytics what drives  a lot of the analytics processing and  marry them in one coherent system  so that basically concludes a rough the  rough overview of titan and faunus um  all of this is released as apache too so  you're happy to check it out  and play around with it it's still very  young we're actively developing on it  there are lots and lots of very  interesting questions that are still  open  and not solved and um you know we  you know would very much appreciate any  input from the community or any help you  want to offer here  um to build those i think it's a  fascinating area there are lots of  really cool questions that you can solve  or address with this  and i yeah i hope you get the time to  check them out  thank you
 

